WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.529
A ceramic lattice can be printed with exquisite

00:00:03.529 --> 00:00:07.378
symmetry. That does not mean it will carry load or

00:00:07.378 --> 00:00:08.917
cooperate with living tissue.

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:21.129
Biomedical ceramic design starts with a difficult

00:00:21.129 --> 00:00:25.333
demand: a manufactured structure must remain useful in

00:00:25.333 --> 00:00:26.910
a biological environment.

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:35.680
Additive manufacturing can produce complex ceramic

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:38.910
forms. Processing, internal architecture and material

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:42.678
properties shape what those forms can do.

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:50.556
Ceramic processing and densification impose constraints

00:00:50.556 --> 00:00:54.697
beyond the original digital model. The final properties

00:00:54.697 --> 00:00:56.250
must be measured.

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:06.067
Mechanical performance and biological response can pull

00:01:06.067 --> 00:01:10.201
design in different directions. An elegant lattice is

00:01:10.201 --> 00:01:12.785
not automatically a medical device.

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:21.632
Porosity, defects, thermal processing and biological

00:01:21.632 --> 00:01:25.344
context constrain performance. None can be inferred

00:01:25.344 --> 00:01:27.995
from an immaculate CAD model.

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:36.358
Clinical application also depends on repeatability,

00:01:36.358 --> 00:01:40.265
sterilisation and regulatory pathways; each requires

00:01:40.265 --> 00:01:42.870
evidence distinct from printability.

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:48.896
The accepted chapter examines translational

00:01:48.896 --> 00:01:53.789
possibilities; it is not a report of a new human

00:01:53.789 --> 00:01:54.279
implantation.
